  <p>These limitations do not apply in case of intent or gross negligence by Sonaria.</p>
  <p>A claim lapses if the Client has not notified Sonaria in writing within 12 months after it became aware, or should reasonably have become aware, of the damage.</p>

  <h2>12. Force majeure</h2>
  <p>Neither party is liable for failure to perform caused by circumstances beyond its reasonable control, such as outages of third-party platforms, internet or power failures, illness, government measures or natural disasters. If force majeure lasts longer than 60 days, either party may terminate the Agreement in writing. Work already performed is paid.</p>

  <h2>13. Term and termination</h2>
  <p>A project Agreement ends when the Deliverables have been delivered and paid. An ongoing Agreement (such as support or a retainer) runs for the period stated in the Proposal and can then be terminated by either party with 30 days' written notice.</p>
  <p>Either party may terminate the Agreement with immediate effect in writing if the other party materially breaches it and does not remedy the breach within 14 days of written notice, or if the other party is declared insolvent or enters liquidation.</p>
  <p>On termination, the Client pays for the work performed and the costs incurred up to the termination date. Obligations that by their nature continue, such as confidentiality, intellectual property and liability, survive termination.</p>

